# #1708 R-P5-1: a bare collection pattern names no runtime type of its own, so it is refused
# (SPY0345) even when the scrutinee is a fully closed collection. `case list()` over a
# `dict[str, int]` scrutinee cannot narrow to any spellable list type, so Sharpy steers to the
# closed spelling rather than emitting a branch that can never run. Python would silently
# never-match. Reification (#1708) subsumes the older cross-collection SPY0361 refusal: the
# pattern is rejected on its own openness before scrutinee compatibility is ever consulted.

def main() -> None:
    d: dict[str, int] = {"a": 1}

    match d:
        case list():
            print("never a list")
        case _:
            print("dict")
